TI  - Can Large Language Model Agents Simulate Human Trust Behavior?
AU  - Feiran Jia
AU  - Ziyu Ye
AU  - Shiyang Lai
AU  - Kai Shu
AU  - Jindong Gu
AU  - Adel Bibi
AU  - Ziniu Hu
AU  - David Jurgens
AU  - James Evans
AU  - Philip H.S. Torr
AU  - Bernard Ghanem
AU  - Guohao Li
AU  - Chengxing Xie
AU  - Canyu Chen
PY  - 2024
AB  - Large Language Model (LLM) agents have been increasingly adopted as simulation tools to model humans in social science and role-playing applications. However, one fundamental question remains: can LLM agents really simulate human behavior? In this paper, we focus on one critical and elemental behavior in human interactions, trust, and investigate whether LLM agents can simulate human trust behavior. We first find that LLM agents generally exhibit trust behavior, referred to as agent trust, under the framework of Trust Games, which are widely recognized in behavioral economics. Then, we discover that GPT-4 agents manifest high behavioral alignment with humans in terms of trust behavior, indicating the feasibility of simulating human trust behavior with LLM agents. In addition,  we probe the biases of agent trust and  differences in agent trust towards other LLM agents and humans. We also explore the intrinsic properties of agent trust under conditions including external manipulations and advanced reasoning strategies. Our study provides new insights into the behaviors of LLM agents and the fundamental analogy between LLMs and humans beyond value alignment. We further illustrate broader implications of our discoveries for applications where trust is paramount.
UR  - https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/1cb57fcf7ff3f6d37eebae5becc9ea6d-Abstract-Conference.html
