TY  - JOUR
TI  - Decoupling Semantic Similarity from Spatial Alignment for Neural Networks.
AU  - Tassilo Wald
AU  - Constantin Ulrich
AU  - Gregor Köhler
AU  - David Zimmerer
AU  - Stefan Denner
AU  - Michael Baumgartner
AU  - Fabian Isensee
AU  - Priyank Jain
AU  - Klaus H. Maier-Hein
PY  - 2024
AB  - What representation do deep neural networks learn? How similar are images to each other for neural networks? Despite the overwhelming success of deep learning methods key questions about their internal workings still remain largely unanswered, due to their internal high dimensionality and complexity. To address this, one approach is to measure the similarity of activation responses to various inputs.Representational Similarity Matrices (RSMs) distill this similarity into scalar values for each input pair.These matrices encapsulate the entire similarity structure of a system, indicating which input lead to similar responses.While the similarity between images is ambiguous, we argue that the spatial location of semantic objects does neither influence human perception nor deep learning classifiers. Thus this should be reflected in the definition of similarity between image responses for computer vision systems. Revisiting the established similarity calculations for RSMs we expose their sensitivity to spatial alignment. In this paper we propose to solve this throughsemantic RSMs, which are invariant to spatial permutation. We measure semantic similarity between input responses by formulating it as a set-matching problem. Further, we quantify the superiority ofsemanticRSMs overspatio-semanticRSMs through image retrieval and by comparing the similarity between representations to the similarity between predicted class probabilities.
UR  - https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/56bd21259e28ebdc4d7e1503733bf421-Abstract-Conference.html
